WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.913 --> 00:00:09.655 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Welcome to Nigeria's states, part 1 of 2. First, I clear the chosen names for
a fresh regional lesson.

2
00:00:10.306 --> 00:00:20.964 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
We work through 20 of the Nigeria regions the map names, once each: where it
sits, one fact worth keeping, and a hook to remember it by.

3
00:00:21.642 --> 00:00:23.383 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Sokoto into view.

4
00:00:32.067 --> 00:00:44.780 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Sokoto sits in the far north-west of Nigeria, where the country runs up into
the Sahel, with Kebbi to the west and Zamfara to the east and its capital
Sokoto in the middle of the dry scrubland.

5
00:00:45.431 --> 00:01:01.775 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Its name comes from the Arabic word suq, a market, and it gave its name to the
Sokoto Caliphate, founded in 1804 by Usman dan Fodio, which was the largest
state in Africa in the nineteenth century.

6
00:01:02.431 --> 00:01:09.165 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the market that became an empire, in the far north-west corner where
the dry Sahel begins.

7
00:01:09.986 --> 00:01:12.749 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Katsina into view.

8
00:01:19.061 --> 00:01:30.149 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Katsina lies in the north of Nigeria, in the dry belt above the Niger river,
touching Jigawa, Kano, Kaduna and Zamfara, with the city of Katsina at its
heart.

9
00:01:30.802 --> 00:01:47.686 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
It was one of the seven original Hausa city-states and a centre of Islamic
learning that rivalled Timbuktu in the sixteenth century, and its mud-built
Gobarau minaret is among the oldest surviving structures in West Africa.

10
00:01:48.369 --> 00:01:56.902 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the mud minaret of the dry north, raised by a city-state that
rivalled Timbuktu as a place of learning.

11
00:01:57.606 --> 00:01:59.347 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Zamfara into view.

12
00:02:05.205 --> 00:02:17.314 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Zamfara lies in the north-west of Nigeria, tucked between Kebbi, Sokoto,
Katsina, Kaduna and Niger, with its capital Gusau in the middle of the dry
northern plain.

13
00:02:17.978 --> 00:02:27.661 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
In 1999 it became the first Nigerian state to adopt sharia criminal law, and
eleven other states have followed since.

14
00:02:28.309 --> 00:02:36.320 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the first state to bring sharia law into its courts, and the line
between the Muslim north and the rest of the country.

15
00:02:37.002 --> 00:02:38.790 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Yobe into view.

16
00:02:44.911 --> 00:02:55.790 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Yobe lies in the far north-east of Nigeria, on the edge of the Sahel, between
Borno to the east and Bauchi to the south, with Damaturu in the middle of the
scrub.

17
00:02:56.439 --> 00:03:07.480 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
It was split from Borno in 1991 and takes its name from the Yobe river, which
forms part of the border with the country of Niger.

18
00:03:08.142 --> 00:03:17.825 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the far north-east corner, split away from Borno, where the wetlands
on its edge are a vital stop for birds crossing the Sahara.

19
00:03:18.515 --> 00:03:20.686 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Jigawa into view.

20
00:03:26.061 --> 00:03:35.871 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Jigawa lies in the north of Nigeria, between Kano and Yobe, with its capital
Dutse, on the dry plains above the Hadejia river.

21
00:03:36.530 --> 00:03:48.384 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Its name comes from a Hausa word for sandy soil, which describes most of it,
and split from Kano in 1991 it is one of the most rural states in the country.

22
00:03:49.044 --> 00:03:59.110 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the sandy soil state, one of the most rural in Nigeria, where a
single river waters rice and wheat in dry country.

23
00:03:59.835 --> 00:04:01.925 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Borno into view.

24
00:04:08.515 --> 00:04:22.459 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Borno occupies the far north-east of Nigeria, where the country runs up into
the Sahel, with Yobe and Gombe along its western side and Maiduguri as its
largest city.

25
00:04:23.121 --> 00:04:36.121 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Its history comes from the Kanem-Bornu empire, whose dynasty ruled for about a
thousand years, and Lake Chad on its border has shrunk by about ninety per
cent since the 1960s.

26
00:04:36.780 --> 00:04:44.408 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the thousand-year empire in the far north-east, and the great lake on
its border that has almost disappeared.

27
00:04:45.095 --> 00:04:47.092 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Kebbi into view.

28
00:04:53.242 --> 00:05:04.074 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Kebbi lies in the far north-west of Nigeria, west of Sokoto and Zamfara, with
its capital Birnin Kebbi, in the dry land along the Niger river.

29
00:05:04.731 --> 00:05:18.802 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
It is named for the old Kebbi kingdom, and the Argungu fishing festival there
brings thousands of men into the river at once with hand nets and gourds, a
truce tradition dating from 1934.

30
00:05:19.476 --> 00:05:30.482 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the north-west rice state, where thousands of men fish the river
together and the festival has held the peace since 1934.

31
00:05:31.174 --> 00:05:33.043 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Kano into view.

32
00:05:38.629 --> 00:05:50.146 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Kano sits in the north of Nigeria, between Katsina and Jigawa, with the city
of Kano at its centre, above the Niger river and out on the edge of the Sahel.

33
00:05:50.803 --> 00:06:02.831 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
It was the greatest trans-Saharan trade city in Hausaland, walled since the
eleventh century, and its dye pits at Kofar Mata have been worked since 1498.

34
00:06:03.491 --> 00:06:10.480 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the walled market city of the north, trading across the Sahara and
dyeing cloth since 1498.

35
00:06:11.333 --> 00:06:13.423 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Bauchi into view.

36
00:06:19.778 --> 00:06:28.694 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Bauchi lies in the north of Nigeria, west of Gombe and Yobe and above the
Benue, with its capital Bauchi in the middle of the uplands.

37
00:06:29.348 --> 00:06:45.172 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Yankari game reserve here holds Nigeria’s largest surviving population of
elephants, and the state was the home of Sir Abubakar Tafawa Balewa, the
country’s first prime minister.

38
00:06:45.834 --> 00:06:52.823 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the elephants of Yankari and the first prime minister, in the north
where the Benue river turns east.

39
00:06:53.547 --> 00:06:56.310 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Gombe into view.

40
00:07:04.834 --> 00:07:15.283 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Gombe is a small state in the north-east of Nigeria, between Bauchi and Borno,
with the town of Gombe at its centre, on the edge of the Sahel.

41
00:07:15.939 --> 00:07:27.282 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
It was founded by a Fulani leader during the jihad of the early 1800s and
split from Bauchi in 1996, one of the last states to be created.

42
00:07:27.958 --> 00:07:38.024 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the young state in the north-east, split from Bauchi in 1996, whose
hills give it more relief than the plains around it.

43
00:07:38.939 --> 00:07:41.191 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Kaduna into view.

44
00:07:52.421 --> 00:08:04.275 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Kaduna lies in the middle-north of Nigeria, a long state running north from
the Niger river, touching Zamfara, Kano, Katsina and the Federal Capital
Territory.

45
00:08:04.934 --> 00:08:18.843 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
The British made it the capital of the Northern Region in 1917, and its name
comes from the Hausa kadduna, the plural of kada, a crocodile, because the
river was full of them.

46
00:08:19.494 --> 00:08:28.027 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the crocodile river, the colonial capital of the north, sitting on
the fault line between the Muslim north and the Christian south.

47
00:08:28.758 --> 00:08:30.674 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Niger into view.

48
00:08:37.247 --> 00:08:49.658 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Niger state lies in the west of central Nigeria, the largest state in the
country by area, reaching north to Kebbi and south to Kwara, with Minna in the
middle.

49
00:08:50.316 --> 00:09:00.893 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
It is named for the Niger river, whose own name probably comes from a Tuareg
phrase meaning river of rivers, and it holds both the Kainji and Shiroro dams.

50
00:09:01.550 --> 00:09:11.999 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the biggest state on the map, named after the river that gives the
country its name, with two great dams holding the water back.

51
00:09:12.733 --> 00:09:15.113 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Plateau into view.

52
00:09:21.184 --> 00:09:32.446 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Plateau state lies in the middle of Nigeria, between the Benue and the Niger
rivers, bordered by Bauchi, Kaduna, Nasarawa and Taraba, with Jos as its
capital.

53
00:09:33.107 --> 00:09:49.315 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
It is named for the Jos Plateau, which rises to about 1,200 metres, and the
Nok culture nearby produced terracotta sculpture from around 500 BC, the
oldest figurative art in West Africa.

54
00:09:49.975 --> 00:09:59.275 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the cool highland in the middle of the hot country, where the British
built a hill station and the oldest clay figures in West Africa came out of
the ground.

55
00:09:59.962 --> 00:10:02.470 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Adamawa into view.

56
00:10:06.868 --> 00:10:17.747 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Adamawa lies in the east of Nigeria, above the Benue and up against the
mountains of Cameroon, bordered by Borno, Gombe and Taraba, with Mubi as its
largest city.

57
00:10:18.408 --> 00:10:33.466 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
It is named for Modibbo Adama, the Fulani leader who founded an emirate here
in the early 1800s, and its terraced Sukur cultural landscape of fields and
stone paths is a UNESCO world heritage site.

58
00:10:34.160 --> 00:10:41.532 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the terraced hills of Sukur in the east, where mountain communities
held out against both the jihad and the colonists.

59
00:10:42.230 --> 00:10:44.146 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Kwara into view.

60
00:10:49.119 --> 00:11:01.786 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Kwara lies in the west of Nigeria, in the middle belt, bordered by Ekiti,
Kogi, Niger, Osun and Oyo, with Ilorin in the middle.

61
00:11:02.442 --> 00:11:14.296 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Its name comes from a Nupe word for the Niger river, and Ilorin was a Yoruba
town taken into the Sokoto Caliphate, which is why it is Muslim and
Yoruba-speaking at once.

62
00:11:14.959 --> 00:11:24.978 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the meeting point of north and south, a Yoruba city under a
caliphate, standing on the seam between the two halves of Nigeria.

63
00:11:25.775 --> 00:11:28.283 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Federal Capital Territory into view.

64
00:11:38.582 --> 00:11:50.947 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
The Federal Capital Territory is the federal district in the middle of
Nigeria, carved out in 1976 and ringed by Kaduna, Kogi, Nasarawa and Niger,
with Abuja inside it.

65
00:11:51.605 --> 00:12:08.498 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
It is not a state in the ordinary sense but the seat of the federal
government, which moved there from Lagos in 1991, and Aso Rock, a 400 metre
monolith, stands behind the presidential complex.

66
00:12:09.163 --> 00:12:19.229 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the federal district that is not a state, the neutral capital chosen
for the middle of the country, with the great rock behind the presidential
offices.

67
00:12:20.110 --> 00:12:22.711 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Nasarawa into view.

68
00:12:36.409 --> 00:12:46.986 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Nasarawa lies in the middle of Nigeria, south of Kaduna and east of the
Federal Capital Territory, bordered by Benue, Kogi, Plateau and Taraba, with
Lafia in the middle.

69
00:12:47.643 --> 00:13:05.283 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Its name comes from the Arabic nasara, meaning victory or help, given by a
nineteenth-century emir, and split from Plateau in 1996 it holds a large share
of Nigeria’s solid minerals, including barite and lithium.

70
00:13:05.947 --> 00:13:15.920 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the state of victory, split from the Plateau in 1996, with high
waterfalls and a great share of the country’s minerals.

71
00:13:17.131 --> 00:13:19.639 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Oyo into view.

72
00:13:36.404 --> 00:13:47.236 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Oyo lies in the south-west of Nigeria, inland from the coast, bordered by
Kwara, Ogun and Osun, with Ibadan in the middle of the farmland.

73
00:13:47.909 --> 00:14:00.274 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
It is named for the Oyo Empire, the dominant Yoruba power from the 1600s to
the 1830s, and Ibadan was the largest city in tropical Africa for much of the
twentieth century.

74
00:14:00.937 --> 00:14:09.250 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the old Yoruba empire, and the great inland city that grew so fast it
outgrew everything around it.

75
00:14:09.998 --> 00:14:12.506 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Taraba into view.

76
00:14:27.983 --> 00:14:41.288 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Taraba lies in the east of Nigeria, running up from the Benue to the border
with Cameroon, bordered by Adamawa, Bauchi, Benue, Gombe, Nasarawa and
Plateau, with Jalingo in the middle.

77
00:14:41.945 --> 00:14:56.237 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
The Mambilla Plateau inside it is the highest land in Nigeria, above 1,800
metres, with tea plantations and a temperate climate, and it is one of the
most ethnically diverse states in the country.

78
00:14:56.901 --> 00:15:03.501 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the high tea country in the east, where the mountains are cool and
the peoples are many.

79
00:15:04.252 --> 00:15:06.121 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Benue into view.

80
00:15:15.667 --> 00:15:33.847 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Benue lies in the middle-south of Nigeria, a long valley following the river
of the same name east towards Cameroon, bordered by Cross River, Ebonyi,
Enugu, Kogi, Nasarawa and Taraba, with Makurdi in the middle.

81
00:15:34.506 --> 00:15:45.512 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
It is called the food basket of the nation because it grows a large share of
Nigeria’s yams, and the Tiv people who live here were traditionally organised
without chiefs.

82
00:15:46.176 --> 00:15:53.850 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the food basket in the river valley, the yams of Benue, and the Tiv
people who had no chiefs.

83
00:16:05.310 --> 00:16:33.570 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
20 regions of Nigeria, one for each target of the map: Sokoto, Katsina,
Zamfara, Yobe, Jigawa, Borno, Kebbi, Kano, Bauchi, Gombe, Kaduna, Niger,
Plateau, Adamawa, Kwara, Federal Capital Territory, Nasarawa, Oyo, Taraba and
Benue.

84
00:16:34.226 --> 00:16:44.036 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the shape of Nigeria from the inside: the regions nest around each
other, and every name on the map belongs somewhere in this one country.
